+<code>^0.18.3</code>
+</li>
+<li>
+<code>^0.19.1</code>
+</li>
+<li>
+<code>^0.20.4</code>
+</li>
+<li>
+<code>&#62;=0.21.1</code>
+</li>
+</ul>
+</dd>
+<dt id="unaffected">Unaffected</dt>
+<dd>
+<ul>
+<li>
+<code>&#60;0.14.0</code>
+</li>
+</ul>
+</dd>
+</dl>
+<h3 id="description">Description</h3>
+<p>Iterating over malformed or truncated IPv6 extension headers can cause
+out-of-bounds reads when the packet was parsed using one of the lax parsing
+functions (e.g. via <code>Ipv6ExtensionsSlice::from_slice_lax</code> or <code>LaxSlicedPacket</code>).</p>
+<p>Lax parsing stops at the first invalid or truncated extension header and returns
+the headers parsed up to that point. The &quot;next header&quot; field of the last returned
+header can then refer to a header that is not part of the slice.
+<code>Ipv6ExtensionSliceIter</code> followed it without checking the remaining length.</p>
+<p>A crafted or truncated IPv6 packet can cause a crash or expose memory located
+behind the packet to the application. Users who do not iterate over the IPv6
+extension headers of laxly parsed packets are not affected.</p>
+<p>As a workaround, do not iterate over the IPv6 extension headers if lax parsing
+reported an error.</p>
